BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ultraxmadeira25k-w14-s3-legs@garmin-training
DTSTAMP:20260908T124212Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261006
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261007
SUMMARY:◆ Legs ONLY\, no upper
DESCRIPTION:Legs ONLY\, no upper - heavy eccentric. Three quad lifts doin
 g three different jobs: HEAVY BILATERAL trap-bar deadlift 4 x 4-6 at 85 
 kg (18 Aug: 75x6x3 with 4+ reps in reserve\, so 1RM is nearer 100 - auto
 regulate to 2 REPS IN RESERVE and add 5 kg when the last set still feels
  easy)\, LOADED UNILATERAL Bulgarian split squat 3 x 8/leg with dumbbell
 s\, and TEMPO ECCENTRIC step-downs 3 x 8-10/leg with 3-4 s lowering. The
 n Nordics 3 x 5 and single-leg calf raises. Never to failure. 48-72 h be
 fore the long day. On a -15% descent hip drop is 23.7 mm vs 7.6 mm uphil
 l (very large effect)\, so descending is a frontal-plane hip problem and
  not only a quad problem - loaded Bulgarians carry that demand\, which i
 s why standalone side planks and cable abduction came out. HYROX SLED TE
 CHNIQUE at the end\, 8-10 x 12.5 m at 50-65% load (~100 kg)\, 60-90 s re
 st. TECHNIQUE ONLY\, never to fatigue. Forearms hooked on the posts\, 30
 -45 deg lean\, short choppy steps 20-30 cm at 3-4 per second\, and never
  let the sled stop. Sled push is PURELY CONCENTRIC\, so it costs your de
 scent block nothing - which is why it is the one Hyrox station you can t
 rain now. At 56 kg you need 1.03x bodyweight of horizontal force to brea
 k a stationary sled versus 0.81x to keep one rolling\, so learning not t
 o stop is worth more to you than to anyone heavier. About 8 minutes. LOW
 ER-LEG CAPACITY\, about 4 min at the end: tibialis anterior raises 3 x 1
 5-20 (heels on a step\, toes lifting)\, heel walks 2 x 20 m\, and eccent
 ric dorsiflexion 3 x 10 slow against a band. The plan had NOTHING for th
 e anterior lower leg and that is the tissue that failed on the 7 Sep tim
 e trial - it is also what controls the foot down on every descending ste
 p\, which is 2\,003 m of Madeira. Progress the load week to week. Keep t
 he soleus and calf work as well\; this is added capacity\, not a substit
 ution.\n\nWeek 14 of 17 - Peak\nTERRAIN WEEKEND #2 - POSBANK AGAIN\, not
  the Ardennes. Roughly EUR 30 of petrol against EUR 200\, and twenty rep
 s of a 90-110 m climb is 1\,800 m of ascent AND hard-surface descent - c
 lose to the whole race. Sustained descending on firm ground is the one t
 hing stairs cannot replicate\, and it is what decides your day. POLES: b
 ought 18-20 Sep\, so you have had three weeks on them by now. Use them t
 he whole session. They cut RPE 14-18% and measurably reduce muscle damag
 e over a climb-and-descent day - but an inexperienced pole user in a fie
 ld study actually got SLOWER\, so the practice is the point. KIT-CHECK H
 ABIT STARTS HERE: every food item must be labelled with its calorie coun
 t\, and all disposable packaging must carry your bib number. Tape a stri
 p on your gel flask reading contents and total kcal (e.g. '4x Upfront Ge
 l PRO - 640 kcal'). Do it from this session on\, not at registration.\n2
 6 days to Ultra X Madeira 25K.
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
