END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ultraxmadeira25k-w17-s0-easy@garmin-training
DTSTAMP:20260908T124212Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261026
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261027
SUMMARY:Easy run 50 MIN (7 km)
DESCRIPTION:Easy run 50 MIN - race week. Volume is down 59% from the week
  15 peak\, which is the middle of the 41-60% band the taper meta-analysi
 s supports\, and FREQUENCY IS HELD AT THREE because cutting frequency is
  the one thing the evidence says not to do. Keep the intensity\, lose th
 e volume. HR CEILING 155 - HARD RULE\, not a talk test. You already fixe
 d this: since 24 Aug your easy running is about 80% below 160 bpm\, up f
 rom roughly 50% over June to mid-August. The number is here to hold the 
 habit\, not to correct you. Novice guidance is about 85% genuinely easy.
  If holding 155 means walking the bridges\, walk them - pace is the outp
 ut here\, not the target.\n\nWeek 17 of 17 - Race\nKeep HR under 158. Wa
 lk the climbs. Talk test.\nRACE WEEK. Sunday 1 Nov\, Calhau de Sao Jorge
  to Machico. THE START IS 11:00\, NOT DAWN - sunset is 18:17 and civil t
 wilight ends 18:41. A 6:30 finish is daylight\; 7:00 is dusk\; 7:30 is D
 ARK on exposed cliff paths. YOUR HEADTORCH IS SAFETY KIT\, NOT A FORMALI
 TY - carry it charged. The late start also gives you an unhurried fuelli
 ng morning: eat properly\, this is an advantage. CHECKPOINTS ARE WATER O
 NLY - the 'water and snacks' line is wrong. Carry 420-490 g of carbohydr
 ate yourself (~13 gels plus mix) across three ~2h10 legs\, roughly 2.6 k
 g of vest. Only ~2 checkpoints\, ~10 km apart. KIT: 1.5 L hydration capa
 city\, taped-seam hooded waterproof\, emergency blanket\, 800 kcal reser
 ve\, phone with GPX\, spare warm layer\, and a BEAKER of at least 15 cl 
 - bottles and flasks are explicitly not accepted. Checks are enforced. C
 ALIBRATION from the 2025 field (n=34): winner 3:02:21\, median 5:24:37 (
 13.0 min/km)\, last finisher 8:03:43\, cut-off 11.5 h. Your target is 6:
 15-6:45. The 25K is the final 25 km of the 50K route - the organiser gav
 e the SHORT race the STEEPEST ground\, 80 m/km against 63.5 for the 50K.
  PACING - the single strongest result in the literature (n=23\,207): in 
 91% of races\, slower finishers overspent in the FIRST segment\; after t
 he opening everyone paces the rest alike. The damage is done at the star
 t. Hike every gradient above 15% from the gun. HR: race average 138-150\
 , climb ceiling 155\, descents 120-140. Above 160 for two minutes on a c
 limb\, walk. Your 17 km run averaged 163 - you cannot hold that for six 
 hours. THE DESCENT IS THE RACE. Racing the first steep descent saves ~10
  minutes and costs 17-46 minutes later: 3:1 to 5:1 against\, before you 
 count the fall risk. And it will never feel hard - VO2 on descents is on
 ly 79% of threshold versus 100% uphill\, so your breathing will tell you
  nothing. That is the trap. Short steps\, 185-195 spm\, gaze 4-5 m ahead
  on the second upcoming foothold\, walk the stone staircases\, zig-zag t
 he wet sections. IN-RACE DIAGNOSTIC: compare climb rate per heartbeat on
  the second climb against the first. More than 5% worse means you overco
 oked the descent - concede ten minutes immediately and save the day. FUE
 L: 60-75 g/h at 2:1 glucose:fructose\, drink to thirst\, skip the salt c
 apsules (no evidence they prevent hyponatraemia or cramp). Carb load 8-1
 0 g/kg for 36-48 h = 450-560 g. Caffeine ~100 mg at 3 h and 75-100 mg at
  4.5 h\, 150-200 mg total - do not scale from 70 kg examples. WEATHER: u
 se Santana on the north coast\, not Funchal - 18 C max\, 110 mm of rain 
 across ~40% of days. Wet basalt and hypothermia are the risks here\, not
